Committed. Here's what landed.

## What changed

`scripts/measure_abstentions.py` — two defects, one gap each, as @yuanhao scoped it.

(a) A real parser. main() had none — it special-cased --test and opened every other argv entry as a path, so --help died with FileNotFoundError: '--help'. Now argparse: --help prints usage and exits 0, --test is a flag on the same parser, positional paths keep their meaning, --since-sha and --since are mutually exclusive.

(b) The eligibility boundary. `classify_eligibility(session_ts, boundary_ts)` — pure, three states, none folded into another:

• INELIGIBLE — strictly before the boundary. Out of numerator and denominator.
• ELIGIBLE — at or after. The boundary is inclusive, and I pinned both sides of that discriminator, not just the side that blocks.
• UNKNOWN_AGE — no parseable timestamp. Not eligible, not ineligible: its own bucket, counted and printed. A silently-absorbed unknown inside my own meter is the exact thing this tool exists to stop.

Session age comes from the day-N-YYYYMMDDTHHMMSSZ directory name (evolve.sh:3471) — no git, works on a bare audit-log checkout. --since-sha shells git show -s --format=%cI at the I/O call site; an unresolvable sha or unparseable stamp refuses to grade rather than quietly becoming "no boundary".

## Why it mattered

The first real run said of 16 gradeable sessions, the gate fired in 0 — and all 16 predated the #808 fix. A firing was impossible by construction. That isn't a 0% hit rate, it's "could not fire" wearing "did not fire"'s clothes, and it gets worse-looking as pre-fix history piles up. Same defect the tool already fixed once for contamination, sitting in the other half of the fraction.

## Verified

• no-flag output byte-identical to before (diffed against a captured baseline, and pinned by a self-test asserting the pre-change verdict string verbatim)
• --help exits 0; --test prints all self-tests passed
• --since and --since-sha both drive the three states end-to-end on a fixture
• MIN_GRADEABLE_SESSIONS = 4 refusal untouched — with a boundary it's now the common case, which is correct
• cargo fmt --check, clippy -D warnings, build, test (7 binaries) all green
• `extract_trajectory.py --test` still passes

I did not touch classify_line, classify, is_quoted_prose or the anchored matching. One gap, not a rewrite.

CLAUDE.md's bullet names the flags and the rule, keeps the "what it cannot do" sentence intact, and records the Day-174 0 of 4 as a **superseded reading** rather than deleting it — that number was measured with no boundary, so it was the category error, and erasing it would teach nothing about why.
